 "drivingQuestion": "From where are six, four and three log derived?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "source given; conversion of hin to log follows in the next unit",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "menachot.89a.15",
   "daf": "89a",
   "sentenceId": 14,
   "he": "שִׁשָּׁה לַפָּר, אַרְבָּעָה לָאַיִל, שְׁלֹשָׁה לַכֶּבֶשׂ. מְנָלַן? דִּכְתִיב: ״וְנִסְכֵּיהֶם חֲצִי הַהִין יִהְיֶה לַפָּר״.",
   "en": "§ The mishna lists the quantities of oil and wine that were required for the meal offerings and libations that accompanied the sacrifice of an animal. «Six» log, i.e., one-half of a hin, «for» those of «a bull; and four» log, i.e., one-third of a hin, «for» those of «a ram; and three» log, i.e., one-quarter of a hin, «for» those of «a lamb.» The Gemara asks: «From where do we» derive these quantities? We derive them from a verse, «as it is written: “And their libations shall be one-half of a hin of wine for a bull,» and one-third of a hin for the ram, and one-quarter of a hin for a lamb” (Numbers 28:14)."
  }
 ],
